🔥 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗥𝗲𝘀𝗼𝘂𝗿𝗰𝗲 𝗤𝘂𝗼𝘁𝗮 𝗘𝘅𝗰𝗲𝗲𝗱𝗲𝗱 — 𝗪𝗵𝘆 𝗖𝗮𝗻'𝘁 𝗬𝗼𝘂 𝗗𝗲𝗽𝗹𝗼𝘆 𝗡𝗲𝘄 𝗣𝗼𝗱𝘀?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

👉 “You are deploying an application to Kubernetes, but the deployment fails with "exceeded quota". How will you troubleshoot it?”

In production, I wouldn't immediately delete existing workloads or increase the quota. First, I'd identify which resource limit has been reached.

🔍 𝗠𝘆 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗔𝗽𝗽𝗿𝗼𝗮𝗰𝗵

1️⃣ Check the namespace quota

kubectl get resourcequota -n <namespace>
kubectl describe resourcequota -n <namespace>

Review used versus hard limits for CPU, memory, Pods, PVCs and other quota-controlled resources.

2️⃣ Check LimitRanges

kubectl get limitrange -n <namespace>
kubectl describe limitrange -n <namespace>

A LimitRange can enforce default or maximum resource requests and limits for containers and Pods.

3️⃣ Inspect the failed workload

kubectl describe deployment <deployment-name> -n <namespace>
kubectl get events -n <namespace> --sort-by=.lastTimestamp

Look for quota-related errors, admission failures and resource configuration problems.

4️⃣ Review resource requests

Check the CPU and memory requests defined in your Deployment YAML. Even if actual usage is low, quota accounting for requested resources may prevent new Pods from being admitted.

5️⃣ Identify the correct solution

🔹 Remove genuinely unused workloads if approved.
🔹 Optimize unnecessary CPU and memory requests.
🔹 Review unused PVCs or other quota-consuming resources.
🔹 Request a quota increase if the workload legitimately needs more capacity.

6️⃣ Verify the deployment

kubectl get deployment,pods -n <namespace>
kubectl describe resourcequota -n <namespace>

Confirm the workload is progressing and quota usage remains within the approved limits.

🎯 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴

ResourceQuota controls resource consumption at the namespace level.

A quota error doesn't always mean the cluster has run out of physical resources. The namespace may have reached its configured limit.
